      <floats-group id="article-36345.s10">
        <fig id="article-36345.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Ketogenesis Pathway. This diagram illustrates ketogenesis, the process by which the liver converts excess acetyl-coenzyme A (acetyl-CoA) into ketone bodies. Two molecules of acetyl-CoA are condensed by thiolase to form acetoacetyl-CoA. &#x003b2;-hydroxy-&#x003b2;-methylglutaryl-CoA (HMG-CoA) is then synthesized when HMG-CoA synthase adds a 3rd acetyl-CoA. HMG-CoA lyase subsequently cleaves HMG-CoA to yield acetoacetate. Acetoacetate can undergo spontaneous decarboxylation to form acetone and carbon dioxide or be reduced to D-&#x003b2;-hydroxybutyrate by D-&#x003b2;-hydroxybutyrate dehydrogenase, using reduced nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide (NADH + H&#x0207a;) and regenerating nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide (NAD&#x0207a;). Uthbrian, <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?search=Ketogenesis&#x00026;title=Special:MediaSearch&#x00026;go=Go&#x00026;type=image">Public Domain</ext-link>, via Wikimedia Commons</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Ketogenesis" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
